E=[
("Attila's invasion of Gaul and Italy",451,"451–452","Invasion","Gaul and Italy","Huns at their height","Attila, ruler of the Hunnic Empire stretching from the Caspian to the Rhine, invaded Gaul in 451 and Italy in 452.","The Huns, nomadic horse archers from the steppe, had driven the Goths into the Roman Empire around 376, setting off the migrations that helped bring down the West. In 451 a Roman–Visigothic army under Flavius Aetius fought Attila to a standstill at the Catalaunian Plains. In 452 Attila sacked Aquileia and northern Italian cities — refugees founded settlements in the lagoons that became Venice — but withdrew after meeting Pope Leo I, probably because of plague and famine in his army. He died in 453 on his wedding night, and his empire collapsed.","Attila","Europe","Ancient world"),
("Umayyad conquest of Hispania",711,"711–718","Invasion","Iberia","Seven centuries of Muslim rule in Spain","A Muslim army of Berbers and Arabs under Tariq ibn Ziyad crossed from Morocco and defeated the Visigothic king Roderic.","Tariq landed at the rock named after him, Jabal Tariq (Gibraltar), in 711 and won the Battle of Guadalete, after which most Visigothic cities surrendered within a few years. Al-Andalus became a centre of learning: Córdoba under Abd al-Rahman III was one of Europe's largest and most cultured cities, where Muslims, Christians and Jews translated science and philosophy. The Christian Reconquista ended with the fall of Granada in 1492.","Umayyad_conquest_of_Hispania","Europe","Medieval world"),
("Mongol invasion of Kievan Rus'",1237,"1237–1240","Invasion","Russia and Ukraine","Two centuries of the 'Tatar yoke'","Batu Khan's Mongol armies conquered the Russian principalities and destroyed Kiev in 1240.","Attacking in winter, when frozen rivers served as roads, the Mongols took Ryazan, Vladimir and Moscow and annihilated Kiev, which a papal envoy described years later as a field of skulls. The principalities became vassals of the Golden Horde, paying tribute through princes of Moscow, who grew powerful as tax collectors. In 1480 Ivan III ended tribute after the Great Stand on the Ugra River.","Mongol_invasion_of_Kievan_Rus%27","Europe","Medieval world"),
("Mongol invasions of India",1221,"1221–1327","Invasion","Northwest India","Repelled by the Delhi Sultanate","Mongol armies raided India repeatedly but were defeated by the Delhi Sultanate.","Genghis Khan chased the Khwarazmian prince Jalal ad-Din to the Indus in 1221 but turned back. Later Chagatai Mongol armies besieged Delhi itself in 1299 and 1303; Alauddin Khalji defeated them at Kili, Amroha and Ravi, building the fort of Siri and a large standing army funded by market controls. India was one of the few major regions that resisted Mongol conquest, and Indian rulers welcomed refugees fleeing the Mongols, including scholars from Persia.","Mongol_invasions_of_India","South Asia","Medieval world"),
("Napoleon's invasion of Russia",1812,"June–December 1812","Invasion","Russia","Destroyed Napoleon's Grande Armée","Napoleon invaded Russia with about 600,000 troops; fewer than 100,000 returned.","Russia withdrew east, burning crops (a 'scorched earth' policy). After the bloody Battle of Borodino, Napoleon entered Moscow, but fires destroyed the city and the tsar refused to negotiate. The retreat in a brutal winter, with typhus, starvation and Cossack attacks, destroyed the army. Tolstoy's War and Peace and Tchaikovsky's 1812 Overture remember it.","French_invasion_of_Russia","Europe","Early modern world"),
("Japanese invasion of China and the Nanjing Massacre",1937,"1937–1945","Invasion","China","Start of the war in Asia","Japan launched a full-scale invasion of China in July 1937.","After the Marco Polo Bridge incident, Japanese forces took Shanghai and the Nationalist capital Nanjing, where soldiers massacred tens of thousands to about 300,000 civilians and prisoners and committed mass rape over six weeks. Japan also used chemical and biological weapons, including Unit 731's plague attacks. About 15–20 million Chinese died in the war. Japanese denial of the massacre still strains relations.","Nanjing_Massacre","East Asia","World wars & modern era"),
("German invasion of Poland",1939,"1 September 1939","Invasion","Poland","Start of World War II in Europe","Germany invaded Poland using blitzkrieg tactics; the Soviet Union invaded from the east on 17 September.","Hitler staged a fake Polish attack on a German radio station at Gleiwitz as a pretext. Britain and France declared war on 3 September. Poland was divided under the secret Molotov–Ribbentrop Pact; both occupiers murdered Polish elites, including the Soviet massacre of about 22,000 officers at Katyn. About 6 million Polish citizens, half of them Jews, died during the war.","Invasion_of_Poland","Europe","World wars & modern era"),
("Operation Barbarossa",1941,"22 June 1941","Invasion","Soviet Union","Largest invasion in history","Germany and its allies invaded the Soviet Union with about 3.8 million soldiers.","Stalin ignored warnings, including from spy Richard Sorge. Germans encircled huge Soviet armies and reached the outskirts of Moscow by December, but winter, Soviet reserves from Siberia and overstretched supply lines stopped them. Behind the front, Einsatzgruppen murdered over a million Jews, beginning the Holocaust by bullets. The war in the east caused most of WWII's military deaths.","Operation_Barbarossa","Europe","World wars & modern era"),
("Fall of Singapore",1942,"15 February 1942","Invasion","Singapore","Britain's worst military defeat","Japanese forces conquered Malaya and Singapore, capturing about 80,000 British, Indian and Australian troops.","Japan advanced down the Malay Peninsula on bicycles, and Singapore's guns, mostly facing the sea, could not stop them. Churchill called it the 'worst disaster' in British military history. Many Indian prisoners joined the Indian National Army, later led by Subhas Chandra Bose.","Fall_of_Singapore","Southeast Asia","World wars & modern era"),
("D-Day landings",1944,"6 June 1944","Invasion","Normandy, France","Opened the Western Front","Allied forces landed about 156,000 soldiers on five beaches in Normandy.","Operation Overlord was the largest seaborne invasion in history, using artificial Mulberry harbours and deception (Operation Fortitude) to make Germany expect a landing at Calais. Paris was liberated in August.","Normandy_landings","Europe","World wars & modern era"),
("Soviet invasion of Afghanistan",1979,"1979–1989","Invasion","Afghanistan","Cold War proxy war","The Soviet Union invaded Afghanistan to support its communist government.","The US, Pakistan and Saudi Arabia armed mujahideen fighters, including Stinger missiles. About 15,000 Soviet soldiers and up to 2 million Afghans died, and millions fled. Soviet withdrawal in 1989 contributed to the USSR's collapse; the war's aftermath helped give rise to the Taliban and al-Qaeda.","Soviet%E2%80%93Afghan_War","Central Asia","World wars & modern era"),
("Iraqi invasion of Kuwait and Gulf War",1990,"1990–1991","Invasion","Kuwait and Iraq","US-led coalition war","Saddam Hussein invaded Kuwait in August 1990; a US-led coalition expelled Iraq in early 1991.","Retreating Iraqi forces set fire to over 600 oil wells. India carried out the largest civilian airlift in history, evacuating about 170,000 Indians from Kuwait.","Gulf_War","Middle East","World wars & modern era"),
("US invasion of Iraq",2003,"2003","Invasion","Iraq","Toppled Saddam Hussein","A US-led coalition invaded Iraq, claiming it had weapons of mass destruction.","No WMD were found. The occupation, sectarian civil war and the rise of ISIS killed hundreds of thousands.","2003_invasion_of_Iraq","Middle East","World wars & modern era"),
("Russian invasion of Ukraine",2022,"24 February 2022 – present","Invasion","Ukraine","Largest war in Europe since 1945","Russia launched a full-scale invasion of Ukraine, following its 2014 annexation of Crimea.","Ukraine resisted the attempt to take Kyiv. The war has killed hundreds of thousands and displaced millions.","Russian_invasion_of_Ukraine","Europe","World wars & modern era"),
]
